WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.287
CROSS JOIN creates every possible combination between two row sets.

00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:08.922
The visual example uses two departments and three appointment slots.

00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:11.957
Two times three gives six combinations.

00:00:11.957 --> 00:00:18.792
Cardiology is paired with all three time slots, and Internal Medicine is also paired with all three time

00:00:18.792 --> 00:00:21.047
slots.

00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:25.617
Unlike INNER or LEFT JOIN, CROSS JOIN does not use an ON condition to decide which rows match.

00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:29.302
Every row on the left combines with every row on the right.

00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:37.742
The PostgreSQL example intentionally limits the department list to two departments so the result stays small

00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:39.182
and easy to inspect.

00:00:39.182 --> 00:00:44.687
The appointment slots table has three rows, so the output contains six department-and-time combinations.

00:00:44.687 --> 00:00:51.572
CROSS JOIN can be useful for generating schedules, scenario grids, complete date-product combinations,

00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:56.737
or test cases. But it can also create extremely large results.

00:00:56.737 --> 00:01:02.042
Before using it, calculate the expected size as the number of left rows multiplied by the number of

00:01:02.042 --> 00:01:03.886
right rows.
